From eed4261ab3e44b7ad60dc689d5f7282c74fd7b4a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: hui <vlado.kerando@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 10:06:31 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 24/43] Mod API: item hooks contract and runtime (capability
 item-hooks)

A server mod takes the behaviour of the items it claims (API level 16,
experimental, server mods only): the use of a claimed item in the main
hand (item-events used and used-on-block, told at the tick's end as
block_used is), its blow on an entity (hurt-entity, answered before the
damage lands: the answer replaces it, kept to nothing and four times
vanilla's, and an error cancels nothing), each server tick every claimed
stack of every online player held or worn (inventory-tick, at most 512 a
call so a longer list comes in turns), and the player being hurt while
wearing one (hurt, answered with how much of it the mod's armour takes).
The mod keeps bytes of its own on a stack itself - energy in a tool, its
mode - with item-hooks stack and set-data: a component on the stack, so
it travels with the stack between slots, players and worlds' saves with
no file of its own. Writes ask for a claimed item, a stack of one (a
split copies components to both halves, so charge on a splittable stack
would double), at most 4 KiB and 32 writes a call, and the players'
inventories lent in the server's hooks: outside them an error, not a
trap. A component that imports an interface with none of its functions -
the shape an exported interface's shared types leave in it, as
item-events uses item-hooks' held-stack - asks for no capability now: a
capability is what a mod calls. The SDK re-exports the interfaces, adds
the six hooks to ServerMod with empty defaults, and export_server_mod!
wires them; the reference is regenerated.

Horizon-Forge-Api: item-hooks-9
Co-Authored-By: ZCode AI agent (GLM-5.3)
