From 6e55eed4a78ea70913fb8543ef27994c1ca435c7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: hui <vlado.kerando@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 19:37:15 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 41/43] Mod API: a server mod may carry content (capability
 server-content), part 2: the cells example, tests and docs

The cells example declares its own block: cells:cell, a lodestone's
behaviour under the mod's own id, in data/cells/horizon/block/cell.json -
content and code in the one .hmod, with `registries` beside the other
capabilities in its manifest. The mod claims it as it claims the vanilla
lodestone, in block-entity-blocks and block-use-events (is_cell answers
for both ids), so a placed cell of its own is an energy cell like any
other: it charges, /cell reads it, the menu opens on a right click, and
breaking it says what was lost.

The sandbox's honest bound: TestGame builds no registry overlay (that is
the window's code), so the new tests/server-content.toml checks that the
package loads - the manifest with `registries` parses, the content reads,
the own block is claimed into a block entity that charges - and the mod's
own harness runs the same file through the real packer
(cargo horizon-mod test). The state ids and models are covered by part
1's full-root test instead.

The docs tell the shape: a new section "A server mod's own content" under
Server mods (what the fence allows, how it differs from a content mod -
code in the package, the content loading in every window with the mod on,
blocks living in a saved world as global state ids), the Multiblocks
pointer now names the server mod itself as a source of new blocks beside
a content mod, and horizon-forge's stage table gains row 18.

Verified by the examples' scripts (17 now), the crate's unit tests, the
cells unit tests, and cargo horizon-mod test in the example's folder (11
scripts, the real packer path); not yet verified by hand in the game's
window.

