From b69a7577202901caa6c5476747917dd698b3ed12 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: hui <vlado.kerando@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 22:05:15 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 43/43] Mod API: messages between server mods (capability
 inter-mod), part 2: the bus and the runtime

The runtime carries the mods' message bus, `inter_mod.rs`: what each
running mod exposed, the channels it subscribed and the messages waiting
for the tick's end - all in memory, nothing saved with the world, since a
mod re-exposes and re-subscribes in its `init`. The five host functions
land on it: expose checks its name and version against the manifest's
`provides` (the mod told the player what it serves before it serves it),
providers reads the bus, subscribe caps a mod at 16 channels, and publish
and send-to share the channel rule, the 30,000-byte cap and a 64-message
(256 KiB) budget a call with `network`. A send-to to a mod that cannot
hear messages - stopped, or built without the `inter-mod-events` export -
is an error, not a silent Ok; past 256 messages (512 KiB) waiting for one
mod, new ones are dropped with a line in the log.

`Mods::inter_mod_tick`, called by the game at the tick's end, delivers in
rounds: at most four a tick, so a library's answer reaches its asker
within the same tick, each message one event call's fuel for its hearer;
a mod gets at most 64 messages (512 KiB) a tick, the rest wait for the
next one, and a message never returns to its sender. A mod that stops -
trapped, or reloaded - is taken off the bus with its exposes,
subscriptions and messages.

`Mods::load_for` now reads every package once, then starts the mods this
game runs in the order their needs ask for: providers before the mods
that need them, else the paths' own order, byte for byte as before
without `requires`. The entries keep the paths' order, so the Mods page
and the commands' priority do not move. A started mod whose need no
turned-on mod provides stays off with "needs spin-lib@1; no turned-on mod
provides it" on its row, and a circle of requires names itself. A
provider that runs elsewhere (a server mod in a window's game) still
counts, since it runs where its consumer will.

Verified by cargo test -p horizon-mods --lib (84 passed, twelve of them
the bus's and the start order's own) and the sandbox battery (32 passed,
the examples unchanged). Not yet verified by hand in the game's window.

